1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,390
On the 7th of May 2022, the ice-dammed lake below Shisper Glacier broke through.

2
00:00:06,770 --> 00:00:09,510
Its flood tore down the Hassanabad Gorge,

3
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:12,350
and the Karakoram Highway bridge was gone.

4
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:15,790
Across Gilgit-Baltistan and Khyber Pakhtunkhwa,

5
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:19,650
more than three thousand glacial lakes fill every summer.

6
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:21,850
Some of them could do the same.

7
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:28,430
Pakistan's space agency, SUPARCO, tracks one hundred and thirty-one of them as potentially dangerous,

8
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:30,510
mostly with optical satellites.

9
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:34,170
But in summer, when the lakes fill, the clouds come,

10
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:34,590
too.

11
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:39,390
Its own update in July said cloud cover after the 16th of June made

12
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:41,330
lake changes difficult to assess.

13
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:47,690
Over Attabad Lake alone, five of thirteen summer Sentinel-2 images were mostly cloud.

14
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:50,590
Radar doesn't wait for clear skies.

15
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:53,870
NISAR, the new satellite from NASA and ISRO,

16
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:57,530
sends L-band microwaves down and listens for the echo.

17
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:01,290
Rough ground scatters the energy back, so it looks bright.

18
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:05,250
Calm water acts like a mirror and bounces the signal away,

19
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:09,870
so a lake looks dark, day or night, cloud or clear.

20
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:12,450
But mountains cast shadows, too.

21
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:17,770
Radar looks sideways, so a slope facing away from the satellite falls into radar

22
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:20,310
shadow, and it looks just as dark as water.

23
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:24,970
NISAR passes over each lake in two directions: once heading north,

24
00:01:25,110 --> 00:01:26,190
once heading south.

25
00:01:26,490 --> 00:01:28,630
A lake is dark from both sides.

26
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:31,010
A shadow is dark from only one.

27
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:36,110
So SARtale compares the two, and anything dark from just one side is set

28
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:36,550
aside.

29
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:42,390
For every pass, SARtale reads a six-kilometer window around the lake straight from NASA's

30
00:01:42,270 --> 00:01:44,790
archive without downloading the whole file.

31
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:49,890
It calms the speckle, then marks every pixel at least three decibels darker than

32
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:52,610
the rest of the window in both polarizations.

33
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:56,870
Inside the lake's outline, those pixels become a water area.

34
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:02,210
On the 23rd of October, Attabad held two point zero eight square kilometers.

35
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:03,890
But is that a change?

36
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:08,650
One number can't tell you, so each lake is compared only with its own

37
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:13,390
earlier passes from the same track because a different angle sees the lake differently.

38
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:16,670
The spread of those passes is the lake's own noise,

39
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:21,110
and only a change bigger than twice that noise counts as growing or shrinking.

40
00:02:21,390 --> 00:02:25,910
For Attabad, the change was a quarter of a square kilometer against a noise

41
00:02:25,770 --> 00:02:26,510
of half.

42
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:27,390
Stable.

43
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:31,130
And a pass that looks wrong, like one on the 7th of July that

44
00:02:30,990 --> 00:02:33,370
came back twenty-five decibels too bright,

45
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:35,730
is left out with the reason shown.

46
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:40,330
We also checked ourselves against Sentinel-2 on the days the sky was clear.

47
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:42,470
The agreement was poor.

48
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:47,850
At every lake we tested, radar water and optical water overlapped by less than

49
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:48,590
one-tenth.

50
00:02:49,030 --> 00:02:51,970
Neither one is a perfect reference, and the app says so.

51
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:55,230
This is indicative screening on provisional data,

52
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:56,450
not a warning.

53
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:01,570
Warnings come from PMD, NDMA, GBDMA, and PDMA.

54
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:05,170
In the app, every lake gets one plain status,

55
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:08,510
a chart of every pass, and a one-page brief to print.

56
00:03:08,770 --> 00:03:11,550
And anyone can put forward a lake we aren't watching yet.

57
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:17,310
Moments later, SARtale asks NASA's catalog how often NISAR passes over it.

58
00:03:17,670 --> 00:03:20,250
From the 17th of June to the 4th of August,

59
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:26,010
while clouds limited optical checks, NISAR gave us sixty valid looks across eleven lakes.

60
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:28,190
That is the promise of radar.

61
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:31,470
When clouds close in, the lakes stay in view.

62
00:03:32,090 --> 00:03:36,990
SARtale by Team SARcastic Scientists for NASA Space Apps,

63
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:37,950
Islamabad.
